The function $F$ is a biholomorphism from $\mathbb D$ onto a bounded convex domain $D$, with inverse $g$, and $F'(0)=8/\pi^2$. There is a continuous even function $\lambda:[-1,1]\to[0,\infty)$, positive on $(-1,1)$ and zero at $\pm1$, such that $$\label{eq:lens-domain}
 \overline D=\{v+it:|t|\leq1,\ |v|\leq\lambda(t)\}.$$ The function $\lambda$ is smooth on $(-1,1)$ and satisfies $$\label{eq:lens-width-curvature}
 \lambda'(t)=-\frac2\pi
       \operatorname{arctanh}\!\left(\sin\frac{\pi t}{2}\right),
 \qquad
 \lambda''(t)=-\sec\frac{\pi t}{2}<0.$$ If $\Theta$ is uniform on $[0,2\pi)$, then $$\label{eq:lens-boundary-law}
 F(e^{i\Theta})\ \stackrel{\mathrm{law}}{=}\
 \varepsilon\lambda(T)+iT,$$ where $T$ is uniform on $[-1,1]$ and $\varepsilon$ is an independent uniform sign.

As $\eta\downarrow0$, $$\label{eq:lens-endpoint-asymptotic}
 \lambda(1-\eta)=\frac2\pi\eta\log\frac1\eta+O(\eta).$$ For every $0<a\leq b<\infty$, $$\label{eq:lens-endpoint-ratio}
 \sup_{a\leq c\leq b}
 \left|\frac{\lambda(1-\delta c)}{\lambda(1-\delta)}-c\right|
 \longrightarrow0\qquad(\delta\downarrow0).$$

Let $d\geq1$, $N\geq d$, and let $\mathcal U\subset\mathbb C^d$ be open and contain $0$. Suppose that $f=(f_1,\ldots,f_N):\mathcal U\to\mathbb C^N$ is holomorphic and has the following properties:*

1.  *$f^{-1}(0)=\{0\}$;*

2.  *for an integer $k\geq1$, $$f(z)=H(z)+O(|z|^{k+1})\quad\text{near }0,$$ where each component of $H$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $k$ and $H(z)\ne0$ whenever $z\ne0$;*

3.  *for every $0<s<1$, the set $\{z\in\mathcal U:|f(z)|^2\leq s\}$ is compact in $\mathcal U$.*


*Then, with $\tau=|f|^2$, $$\label{eq:holomorphic-minor-mass}
\int_{\{\tau<1\}}
 \sum_{\substack{I\subset\{1,\ldots,N\}\\|I|=d}}
 \left|\det\left(\frac{\partial f_i}{\partial z_j}\right)_{
                       i\in I,\ 1\leq j\leq d}\right|^2\,dV
\geq \frac{(\pi k)^d}{d!}.$$ All norms are Euclidean. Rows in each determinant are in increasing index order.

Let $b_1,\ldots,b_m$ be nonzero, pairwise nonproportional real rows spanning $(\mathbb R^d)^*$, and let $F,D,\lambda$ be as in Lemma <a href="#lem:planar-lens" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:planar-lens">6</a>. With $A$, $S$, and $\Sigma_X$ defined in <a href="#eq:strip-body" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:strip-body">[eq:strip-body]</a>, <a href="#eq:feasible-probabilities" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:feasible-probabilities">[eq:feasible-probabilities]</a>, and <a href="#eq:feasible-simplex-union" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:feasible-simplex-union">[eq:feasible-simplex-union]</a>, respectively, $$\label{eq:simplex-integral}
 1\leq S
   =\frac{d!}{4^d}\int_A|\Sigma_X|\,dX
   \leq\frac{d!}{4^d}|A|\,|A^\circ|.$$ In particular, $|A|\,|A^\circ|\geq4^d/d!$, and $$\label{eq:simplex-deficit}
 0\leq\int_A\bigl(|A^\circ|-|\Sigma_X|\bigr)\,dX
       \leq |A|\,|A^\circ|-\frac{4^d}{d!}.$$

Let $K\subset\mathbb R^n$, $n\ge1$, be an origin-symmetric convex body with $P(K)=4^n/n!$. Put $Q=K^\circ$, $d=n+1$, and define the lifted bodies $B,C$ by <a href="#eq:segment-lift" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:segment-lift">[eq:segment-lift]</a>. For every $X\in\operatorname{int}B$ and every nonzero $\xi=(q,a)\in\mathbb R^n\times\mathbb R$, put $$T=p_C(\xi)=\max\{p_Q(q),|a|\}.$$ There are $1\le r\le d$ vectors $c_i=(b_i,\sigma_i)$ with $b_i\in Q$ and $\sigma_i\in\{-1,1\}$, nonnegative numbers $\alpha_i$, and a vector $Y\in\mathbb R^d$, such that $$\label{eq:optimal-representation}
 \xi=\sum_{i=1}^r\alpha_i c_i,
 \qquad \sum_{i=1}^r\alpha_i=T,$$ and $$\label{eq:common-witness}
 |h\cdot Y|\le\lambda(h\cdot X)\quad(h\in C),
 \qquad
 c_i\cdot Y=\lambda(c_i\cdot X)\quad(1\le i\le r).$$ In particular, for every finite representation $\xi=\sum_{j=1}^s\beta_jh_j$ with $\beta_j\ge0$ and $h_j\in C$, $$\label{eq:optimal-cost}
 \sum_{i=1}^r\alpha_i\lambda(c_i\cdot X)
 \le \sum_{j=1}^s\beta_j\lambda(h_j\cdot X).$$ The choices may depend on $X$ and $\xi$; the same choice satisfies <a href="#eq:optimal-cost" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:optimal-cost">[eq:optimal-cost]</a> for all the competing representations.
